ASSEMBELY
========================================================================

The device is optimized to be built by hobbyists. Here are some hints
concerning the assembely of the device. 

==POWER ON VIA TEST-SW==
The test SW initiates the Test-Routines when it is pressed within powering
on the device. Normaly you would press the switch, and than you would 
press the connect-key on the terminal. If you want to initiate the test
routines without a terminal hooked to the device you can add two wires
to the free side of the switch and short circuit the power-on-relais with.
Then you initiate the test routines without a terminal.


Note: This modification is strongly recommanded if you wish to modify/develop
      the software on the device.

       
      ________________        
        Bottom-Vidw

            o o
            o o <--+----- Free switch contacts 
            o o <--+



                          |
                  oooo oo |
                  AA   AA |
                  ||   ||
                  ++   |+-----GND
                   |   +------+5V
                   |
               Relais 
           Switch contacts
             

==PROGRAMMING THE DEVICE==
1) Override the standby functions
The first programming of the device is a bit difficult because the device
has a standby-circuit installed. So the first thing to do is to override
the standby functions. If you have made the modification mentiond above
you can just press the test-sw continously. If not you should short cicuit
the Relais-Switch-Contacts:

                          |
                  oooo oo |
                  AA   AA |
                  ||   ||
                  ++   |+-----GND
                   |   +------+5V
                   |
               Relais 
           Switch contacts
             
Now you have a continous powered device and you can proceed with the
programming procedure.

2) Fusebit configuration
Both, the miniBTX as well as the main CPU requie a fuse configuration.
I prefer to set up fusebits with the program ponyprog because it has
a nice grafical frontend to program the fusebits. And when everything
works perfect i usually take a screenshot of the fusebit configuration.

For the miniBTX fusbit configuration see: /miniBTX/doc/fusemap.bmp
For the main CPU fusbit configuration see: /mikroPAD/doc/fusemap.bmp

Note: Do not use "make fuse" command! The fusemap in the makefile is
      obsolete and will cause problems because the brown-out-detection
      is not enabled in this configuration.

To test if the controller had survived the fusebit programming procedure
just try to reread it. If the controller answers, everything should be
ok. If you do not get a response, check if the external clock signal
is present.

Note: The programmer port has the standart atmel UISP pinout. The left
      on of the two ports is for the miniBTX circuit and the right
      one for the main CPU.

Note: If programming with the UISP programmer fails check if the 
      programming speed is set correctly. Imagine that the controller
      runs with the internal, slow oscillator before it is configured
      to use the fast external 16Mhz oscillator.

3) Flashing the firmware
It is recommanded to program the miniBTX controller first. When the 
programming is finished the miniBTX part of the device should work.
You can easyly verify the miniBTX circuit by connecting a terminal
and initiating a dial attempt. You should hear the dialtones and 
your terminal should say "ONLINE" or something. Of cause you will
get a blank screen.

The next thing is a bit more difficult. The atmega2561 CPU is not 
officially supported by the GCC so we used a patched version of
the gcc to compile the sources. However, you do not need to hassle
around with patching and recompiling your gcc - you can use the
ready built .deb packages in /gcc. Just uninstall your courrent
compiler and then install the the .deb packages.

If the sourcecode of for the main-cpu compiles successfully you
should program the device to test if it comes alive. Later you
can do the necessary configurations explained in ./readme.

Note: It is recomanded to use an avr UISP (USB version) to program
      the AVRs. If you have a different programmer you can alter
      the makefile or program the resulting .hex files manualy.

Now your device is ready to boot. If the Left LED or the LED
in the middle lights up continously the RAM-Test failed. If the
right LED is flashing once per second the operating system has
successfully booted. If the RAM-Test fails it is not a problem
the experiance has shown that most of our home made PCBs do not
boot correctly when powered the first time. You must check all
whires now. If everything fails, try to replace the RAM and the
Adress-Latch. Thats the reason why i strongly recomand to mount
the RAM and the Adress-latch in precision sockets.

Note: Homebrew PCBs mostly have problems with short or open 
      circuit. This is normal - so check the connections
      with a multimeter. The connections to the network 
      intrface should be checked excessivly before mounting 
      the RJ45 socket because the coils of the built in
      magnetics look like a short-circuit on the multimeter.

If the system boots and does the dhcp request correctly the most
critical parts are working. Now you are ready to execute the basic
system check. See ==Test procedure== in ./readme for instructions.

The assembely of the device is now finished. now you can go ahead 
with the configuration of the device.


==ORIENTATION==
When mounting the SMD parts to the board you must keep in mind that the
view on the placement plan is mirrored. This is a mistake that is very
often made (i made it when mounting the 74x32/74x00 ics and was wondering
about the high power consumption). So be careful if when you mount the
smd-parts.


==WIREBRIDGES==
The device contains lots of wirebridges. If you have an industrial process
you would drill a hole and mout the wire on the component side. You can
sorten this process by just adding the wirebridges on the component side,
just like if it would be an SMD-Component.


==PRECISION-SOCKETS==
I recommand to use precesion sockets - especially if you need the device for
developing proposes. Precesion sockets make it easyer to change suspected
defective parts.


==FIRST POWER ON==
The first power on should be done with a laboratory power supply that limits
the courrent if something strange happens. Set the courrent limit to about
300mA. That is the maximum power the device should consume. To avoid damage
of parts you should place a finger of every part on the board doing this
you will notice if parts get hot. If you feel pain in your fingers you better
turn of the device and check if everything is correctly soldered.

There also a nother hint. Just solder the main CPU (ATmega256) and the ISP
connector first. Than check if the CPU can be programmed. If it responds to
the programmer you know that the CPU is working. 





